From: OpenWrt fork local <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: util-linux: initialize three locals that GCC cannot prove are set

Three -Wmaybe-uninitialized warnings appear when util-linux 2.41.5 is built
with gcc. All three are flow-limitation false positives, not live bugs, and
each is fixed by its declaration rather than by a warning flag:

lib/mbsedit.c:228 'ncells'
  mbs_edit_insert() only reads ncells after mbs_insert() returned something
  other than (size_t)-1, and mbs_insert() (same file, line 152) assigns
  *ncells on every path that does not return -1: "#else" sets it to 1, and
  the HAVE_WIDECHAR path sets it from wcwidth() before falling through. The
  single "goto out" that skips the assignment is the -1 return the caller
  rejects.

libblkid/src/superblocks/btrfs.c:208 'wp'
  sb_log_offset() reaches "*bytenr_ret = wp" only when sb_write_pointer()
  returned 0 or -ENOENT. sb_write_pointer() (line 97) sets *wp_ret at line
  131 immediately before its -ENOENT return and at line 164 before its 0
  return; its -EIO and -EUCLEAN returns are caught by the caller's
  "if (ret != -ENOENT && ret) goto out".

libfdisk/src/dos.c:779 'id'
  dos_set_disklabel_id() reads id only after "if (rc) return -EINVAL", and
  rc can only be non-zero before that point if fdisk_ask_string() failed -
  in which case the "if (!rc)" block that assigns id via strtoul() is
  skipped and the function has already returned.

Initialising the three is a no-op for every reachable path and removes the
diagnostics without hiding any class of warning.

--- a/lib/mbsedit.c
+++ b/lib/mbsedit.c
@@ -215,7 +215,7 @@
 
 int mbs_edit_insert(struct mbs_editor *edit, wint_t c)
 {
-	size_t n, ncells;
+	size_t n, ncells = 0;
 
 	if (edit->cur_bytes + MB_CUR_MAX > edit->max_bytes)
 		return 1;
--- a/libblkid/src/superblocks/btrfs.c
+++ b/libblkid/src/superblocks/btrfs.c
@@ -175,7 +175,7 @@
 	struct blk_zone *zones;
 	int ret;
 	int i;
-	uint64_t wp;
+	uint64_t wp = 0;
 
 
 	zone_size_sector = pr->zone_size >> SECTOR_SHIFT;
--- a/libfdisk/src/dos.c
+++ b/libfdisk/src/dos.c
@@ -739,7 +739,7 @@
 static int dos_set_disklabel_id(struct fdisk_context *cxt, const char *str)
 {
 	char *buf = NULL;
-	unsigned int id, old;
+	unsigned int id = 0, old;
 	struct fdisk_dos_label *l;
 	int rc = 0;
 
